{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-sonnet-5-5", "id": "msg_011CfcTFRSqdB7cuC83W6vny", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "A list is mutable and a tuple is immutable. Nearly every other difference follows from that.\n\n| | **List** | **Tuple** |\n|---|---|---|\n| Syntax | `[1, 2, 3]` | `(1, 2, 3)` (a single item needs a trailing comma: `(1,)`) |\n| Mutability | You can add, remove, or change items in place. | You can't change it after creation. |\n| Methods | `append`, `extend`, `insert`, `remove`, `pop`, `sort`, and others | Only `count` and `index` |\n| Hashable | No, so it can't be a dict key or set member. | Yes, if all its items are hashable. |\n| Memory and speed | Slightly larger and slower | Slightly smaller and faster to create and iterate |\n\n**When to use which**\n- **List:** a collection that grows, shrinks, or is reordered, usually with items of the same kind. Examples are a list of users or a queue of jobs.\n- **Tuple:** a fixed group of values, where position carries meaning. Examples are `(x, y)` coordinates, `(name, age)` records, multiple return values, and dict keys like `{(row, col): value}`.\n\n```python\nnums = [1, 2, 3]\nnums[0] = 99          # fine\n\npoint = (1, 2)\npoint[0] = 99         # TypeError: 'tuple' object does not support item assignment\n```\n\n**Caveat:** a tuple is only shallowly immutable. If it holds a mutable object, that object can still change:\n\n```python\nt = ([1, 2], 3)\nt[0].append(99)       # t is now ([1, 2, 99], 3)\n```\n\nFor tuples with named fields, see `collections.namedtuple` or `typing.NamedTuple`."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 7616, "cache_read_input_tokens": 16975, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 7616}, "output_tokens": 2, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "not_available"}, "i